name c01-overwatch
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# ARMORED FIST (NovaLogic 1994) — CAMPAIGN ONE: "OVERWATCH", ALL SEVEN MISSIONS (`INDIA1…INDIA7`).
# Spec: mods/armored-fist/doc/campaign.md. Substitutions: mods/armored-fist/doc/substitutions.md.
# Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# THE MISSION ORDER IS THE FILE ORDER AND THE FILE ORDER IS THE MENU ORDER. `INDIA1…INDIA7` is how the
# campaign ships and how [GUIDE] chapter 4 walks it. WHAT THE ORIGINAL DOES THAT THIS FILE CANNOT: the
# campaign map gates missions by colour and a RED mission is not selectable until its prerequisite has
# been WON [MAN p.49]. That is a branch, and a `.fbc` is a list. Which of the seven gate which is not
# in any source read, so nothing is asserted about it and the list runs straight through.
#
# NO `time` LINE. Six of the seven declare 0600Z and mission 5 declares 1900Z, because `INDIA5` is the
# campaign's only night mission by its own sky asset (campaign.md §4b). A single campaign clock would
# put Night's Quest in daylight, which is exactly the thing the data settles.
#
# `stop_on never`, because attrition is the point — except that in THIS campaign attrition cannot
# happen: no red unit in Overwatch has a sensor or a weapon (see any mission header, D2), so no blue
# unit can be lost and `carry units` carries nothing but the ground kills. The line is written out so
# the file says what it does, and the emptiness of it is the campaign's headline finding.
#
# THE CALLSIGNS ARE PAIRWISE DISJOINT ACROSS ALL SEVEN FILES (`m1*` … `m7*`), which is what makes the
# carry legible: a T-80 killed in mission 4 can never be confused with one of mission 5.
#
# READING RULE (binding). THE CAMPAIGN'S EXIT CODE IS THE WORST MISSION'S AND IS THEREFORE NOT THE
# CAMPAIGN'S VERDICT. Each `.fbm` names the ONE unit whose UNIT_RESULT decides it; read the seven of
# them out of `campaign MISSION_RESULT` plus each step's `mission RESULT unit=<the named unit>`.
# Measured standalone at the time of writing (goals dead / goals declared):
#   1 Slaughterzone!  exit 3   4/12   base, artillery and two BMPs; six T-80s and Bandits 2-3 untouched
#   2 Night Forger    exit 3   3/12   three BMPs; all seven T-80s and Bandit 3 untouched
#   3 Rubicon         exit 0   2/2    encampment and emplaced artillery, one canister               WON
#   4 Thunderclap     exit 3   4/11   depot and three BMPs; the Mk 84 missed its T-80 by 40.9 m
#   5 Night's Quest   exit 3   2/13   two BMPs; NINE bunkers survived five Mk 84s (62.5 … 70.0 m)
#   6 War Hammer      exit 0   2/2    refuel depot and fuel tanks                                   WON
#   7 Corrosion       exit 3   4/6    comms links, compound and two BMPs; both T-80s stood
# TWO OF SEVEN FLY THEIR OWN CONDITION, and the five that do not fail for exactly two reasons, both
# named in every header: `target_hard` is unreachable by any weapon this tree can deliver at 40 km
# (0 of 29 deliveries inside 17.7 m), and a delivery unit gets ONE pass, so a mission with 11 or 13
# goals cannot service them. NEITHER IS A DEFECT TO REPORT — they are the measurement this campaign
# was built to take.

mission ../missions/c01m01-slaughterzone.fbm
mission ../missions/c01m02-night-forger.fbm
mission ../missions/c01m03-rubicon.fbm
mission ../missions/c01m04-thunderclap.fbm
mission ../missions/c01m05-nights-quest.fbm
mission ../missions/c01m06-war-hammer.fbm
mission ../missions/c01m07-corrosion.fbm
